  "learn": {
    "id": "L-004",
    "title": "Why debt fund values move when bond yields change",
    "paragraphs": [
      "A bond pays a fixed rate of interest. When yields on new bonds fall, older bonds that pay more become more valuable, so their prices rise; when yields rise, their prices fall.",
      "Debt mutual funds value their bonds at market prices every day, so these price changes show up in the NAV. The longer a bond has left to maturity, the more its price changes for the same move in yields.",
      "On Monday the 10-year government bond yield fell by nearly 10 basis points, Business Standard reported. In September, AMFI's chief executive linked part of the fall in industry assets to mark-to-market losses: the same effect in reverse."
    ],
